      mechanism: "Theophylline inhibits phosphodiesterase isozymes, especially PDE3 and to a lesser extent PDE4 at relevant airway effects, which reduces cAMP breakdown and supports bronchodilation. Adenosine-receptor antagonism contributes to CNS, cardiac, gastric, and other effects, while additional anti-inflammatory mechanisms are proposed but not fully established. Hepatic CYP1A2, CYP3A4, and CYP2E1 metabolism creates many interactions. Tobacco or marijuana smoke induces clearance; stopping smoke exposure can sharply raise concentration. Sustained fever, liver dysfunction, heart failure, sepsis, and some inhibitors reduce clearance, allowing accumulation. Concentration-dependent receptor and PDE effects explain why early nausea or tremor can precede arrhythmia and seizure.",
      boxedWarning: "No current U.S. boxed warning. High-yield warning context: theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index and nonlinear elimination can make a small dose increase produce a disproportionate concentration rise. New vomiting, persistent nausea, insomnia, tremor, agitation, tachycardia, irregular rhythm, hypotension, or seizure is toxicity until assessed. Hold further doses, obtain a timed level and metabolic panel, place severe cases on cardiac monitoring, and involve poison control/toxicology for significant exposure.",
