      description: "Acetylcysteine is not a generic respiratory medicine. As an inhaled mucolytic, its free sulfhydryl group reduces disulfide bonds that cross-link mucin proteins, making secretions less viscous but often more voluminous; the patient must be able to cough or be suctioned. As an acetaminophen antidote, it supplies cysteine for hepatic glutathione synthesis and can directly support detoxification of the reactive metabolite NAPQI. Early treatment is most protective, but clinically indicated treatment should not be withheld merely because the classic early window has passed.",
      mechanism: "Mucus gel strength depends partly on disulfide bridges between cysteine residues in mucin. Acetylcysteine donates reducing equivalents through its thiol group, cleaving those bridges and lowering viscosity. In acetaminophen overdose, normal glucuronidation and sulfation saturate, CYP metabolism produces NAPQI, and glutathione depletion allows NAPQI to bind hepatocyte proteins. Acetylcysteine is deacetylated to cysteine, replenishing the rate-limiting substrate for glutathione; it also enhances sulfation and can reduce NAPQI directly. Later benefit includes antioxidant and microcirculatory support. This chemistry explains both the sulfur odor and the two very different bedside uses.",
      boxedWarning: "No current U.S. boxed warning. High-yield warning context: inhaled acetylcysteine can trigger bronchospasm and can suddenly liquefy a large secretion burden, creating obstruction if cough or suction is inadequate. IV acetylcysteine can cause anaphylactoid hypersensitivity with flushing, urticaria, wheeze, hypotension, or angioedema and must be diluted in a weight-appropriate fluid volume. The inhalation/oral solution labeled NOT FOR INJECTION is not interchangeable with the sterile IV product.",
      adverseEffects: ["Inhaled: bronchospasm, chest tightness, rhinorrhea, stomatitis, nausea, unpleasant sulfur odor, and increased liquid secretions", "IV/oral antidote: nausea, vomiting, flushing, urticaria, angioedema, bronchospasm, hypotension, and fluid or electrolyte problems"],
