      description: "Umeclidinium is a once-daily inhaled long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) delivered through the Ellipta dry-powder device for COPD maintenance. It blocks acetylcholine at airway M3 receptors, reducing the vagal smooth-muscle contraction that contributes substantially to COPD airflow limitation. It does not stimulate beta2 receptors and does not suppress airway inflammation like a corticosteroid. The powder contains lactose that may contain milk proteins, so severe milk-protein allergy is a contraindication; lactose intolerance is a digestive enzyme problem and is not the same risk. Because it is maintenance therapy, taking extra actuations during acute distress adds anticholinergic toxicity without providing an appropriate rescue strategy.",
      mechanism: "Airway M3 receptors couple to Gq, phospholipase C, IP3, intracellular calcium release, and myosin light-chain kinase activation. Umeclidinium competitively antagonizes muscarinic receptors and prevents acetylcholine-driven contraction, producing prolonged bronchodilation after inhalation. Its effect is predominantly local, but systemic exposure can still impair bladder detrusor contraction and pupillary/ciliary control. The same receptor blockade explains both benefit and adverse effects: less bronchial tone improves airflow, while excessive blockade can cause dry mouth, urinary retention, and angle-closure symptoms. It has no role in neutralizing histamine, treating anaphylaxis, or immediately reversing acute COPD deterioration.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Do not initiate for acutely deteriorating COPD or use for acute symptoms. Paradoxical bronchospasm, immediate hypersensitivity including anaphylaxis, acute narrow-angle glaucoma, and urinary retention require discontinuation or urgent evaluation. The lactose-containing dry powder is contraindicated in severe milk-protein allergy."),
