      description: "Aclidinium is a twice-daily inhaled long-acting muscarinic antagonist (LAMA) delivered by the breath-actuated Pressair dry-powder device. It blocks airway M3 receptors, reducing acetylcholine-driven smooth-muscle contraction and helping airways remain open between doses. Once drug reaches plasma, ester hydrolysis rapidly converts it to inactive metabolites, which limits systemic persistence but does not eliminate dry mouth, glaucoma, urinary retention, or hypersensitivity risk. Its device provides visual and audible feedback after a successful inhalation, making inspiratory technique part of the dose. Aclidinium is COPD maintenance, not asthma rescue, and the lactose carrier creates a severe milk-protein-allergy restriction.",
      mechanism: "M3 receptors on airway smooth muscle use Gq, phospholipase C, IP3, and intracellular calcium to activate contraction. Aclidinium competitively and reversibly blocks muscarinic receptors, preventing acetylcholine from generating this calcium signal and producing prolonged site-specific bronchodilation. The molecule is a quaternary ammonium ester. In plasma it is rapidly hydrolyzed chemically and by butyrylcholinesterase into inactive alcohol and acid metabolites, reducing duration of systemic exposure compared with the airway receptor effect. This pharmacokinetic feature explains a relatively low systemic burden; it does not change the core antimuscarinic warnings or make extra doses appropriate during acute dyspnea.",
