      usedToTreat: "Maintenance treatment of asthma in label-specified ages and maintenance treatment of COPD at the indicated strength. Under current GINA 2026 and an appropriate prescribed local regimen, low-dose ICS-formoterol can also be used as an anti-inflammatory reliever or maintenance-and-reliever therapy. It is not a universal self-directed rescue product, and the U.S. SYMBICORT label itself still states it is not for relief of acute bronchospasm.",
      description: "Budesonide/formoterol is an inhaled corticosteroid plus rapid-onset long-acting beta2 agonist combination: budesonide suppresses airway inflammation and formoterol rapidly relaxes smooth muscle for about 12 hours. This pairing matters because each symptom-driven dose in an approved AIR or MART plan can treat both bronchoconstriction and the inflammatory flare that a SABA alone leaves behind. Formoterol's fast onset distinguishes this combination from fluticasone/salmeterol. The nurse must reconcile guideline strategy, local approval, product strength, and the patient's written plan rather than assume every Symbicort-like inhaler is rescue or maintenance only. Rinsing and spitting after scheduled exposure reduces local candidiasis.",
      mechanism: "Budesonide diffuses into airway cells, binds cytosolic glucocorticoid receptors, and translocates to the nucleus. It induces anti-inflammatory proteins and represses NF-kappaB/AP-1-driven cytokines, reducing eosinophils, edema, vascular leak, mucus signaling, and hyperresponsiveness over time. It also increases beta2-receptor expression and limits desensitization. Formoterol rapidly activates beta2-Gs-adenylyl cyclase-cAMP-PKA signaling, reducing myosin light-chain kinase and intracellular calcium-dependent contraction. Its membrane depot sustains action. The steroid addresses attack biology while the LABA addresses airway caliber; this causal complementarity explains why ICS-formoterol reliever strategies reduce exacerbations compared with bronchodilator-only relief.",
      boxedWarning: noBoxed("Fixed ICS-LABA treatment avoids LABA monotherapy, but serious mistakes remain possible. Do not use more than the prescribed AIR/MART maximum, do not add another LABA, and do not assume the U.S. product label authorizes acute relief outside a clinician-directed plan. Paradoxical bronchospasm, severe hypersensitivity, adrenal suppression, pneumonia in COPD, beta-agonist cardiovascular effects, or deteriorating disease require reassessment."),
